    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Penetrating stain does not dry on the wood. It dries in the wood. Whatever could not get in is what you are standing in.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-stain-dries" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. How Deck Stain Is Supposed to Dry
        </h2>

        <p>
          It helps to understand what is meant to happen before working out why it did not. A penetrating deck stain — which is most oil-based products and most semi-transparent water-based ones — is a mix of pigment, resin or oil, and a carrier. When you apply it, the carrier pulls the pigment and binder down into the top layer of wood fibre. Then the carrier leaves: mineral spirits evaporates from an oil stain, water evaporates from a water-based one. What is left behind, the linseed or alkyd or paraffin oil in an oil stain or the acrylic in a water-based one, then has to cure. Oils cure by oxidation, reacting with air over a couple of days to form a solid. Acrylics cure by the polymer particles fusing together as the last water leaves.
        </p>

        <p>
          The critical point is that all of this is designed to happen inside the wood, where the binder is spread thin across an enormous surface area of fibre and has plenty of air around it. A film of the same stain sitting on top of the wood is a different thing altogether. It is thick, it has air on one side only, and in the case of oil stains it can take weeks to oxidise through — or never quite manage it, because the surface skins over and seals the wet layer underneath. That is your sticky deck. Nearly every cause below is a variation on "there was stain left on the surface with nowhere to go".
        </p>

        <h2 id="causes" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. The Causes, Most Likely First
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Over-Application</h3>
        <p>
          The number one cause by a wide margin, and the root of most of the others. Someone read "two coats" on the can and flooded the boards, or sprayed until the wood looked uniformly wet and walked away, or figured more stain meant more protection. The wood took what it could hold and the rest stayed on top. Oil stains are the worst offenders because they are slow to skin and the excess stays liquid and mobile for days, migrating into low spots and pooling at board edges. Water-based stains over-applied form a thick, rubbery film that stays soft. If your deck has shiny patches, dark pools along the board edges or a surface you can leave a fingerprint in, this is your cause, and probably your only one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wood That Could Not Absorb It</h3>
        <p>
          The same amount of stain that soaks harmlessly into a weathered deck will sit on top of one that is closed. New smooth lumber carries mill glaze, the compressed, slick skin left by planer knives. Dense species — ipe, cumaru, and to a lesser degree Douglas fir — have little open fibre to begin with. Wood that was previously stained or sealed still carries the old resin and cannot take a fresh full coat. A deck sanded with fine grit has had its pores burnished shut, which is one reason we tell people not to sand finer than 80. In every case the fix for next time is the water test: sprinkle a board and see whether the water soaks in within a minute or beads. If it beads, the stain will too.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cold, Humidity and Still Air</h3>
        <p>
          Both drying mechanisms slow down in the cold. Below about 50 degrees Fahrenheit, mineral spirits evaporates sluggishly and oxidation crawls; a stain that would have set in a day at 75 can still be wet after three at 45. High humidity makes water-based stains almost impossible to dry — they cannot lose water into air that is already full of it — and slows the oil side too. Still air is the third factor: a deck in a sheltered corner with no breeze holds a layer of saturated air right at the surface. A deck stained in late September in the Pacific Northwest or the Great Lakes can stay tacky for a week for this reason alone, even at sensible application rates. Our <a href="/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/">temperature guide</a> covers the windows to aim for.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">No Back-Brushing or Back-Wiping</h3>
        <p>
          When you spray or roll a penetrating stain, it lands unevenly: more in the middle of the pass, less at the edges, heavy in the low spots of a cupped board. Back-brushing — going over each board with a dry brush or pad within a few minutes — spreads the excess into the dry spots, works it down into the grain, and removes what the wood cannot take. Skip it and every place the sprayer lingered becomes a sticky patch. On dense wood and single-coat products, the instructions go further and call for back-wiping with a rag to remove everything that has not soaked in within fifteen or twenty minutes. Skipping that step on ipe or on a product like Ready Seal is the most reliable way to produce a deck that never dries.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Applying Over a Wet Deck</h3>
        <p>
          If the boards were washed the day before, or it rained two days ago and the deck is shaded, the wood is still holding water in its cells. Stain cannot go into a space that is already full. It sits on top, and worse, as the water below tries to leave over the next few days it pushes up through the stain and keeps it from curing. Wood needs 48 hours of dry weather after washing, or a moisture meter reading under 15 per cent, before stain goes on. The full sequence is in our <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">deck prep guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Wrong Product for What Is Already There</h3>
        <p>
          Oil-based stain applied over a sound water-based acrylic finish, or over any film former, has nothing to penetrate into. The acrylic is a plastic skin, and the oil simply sits on it and stays greasy. The same happens in reverse with some combinations, and with any stain applied over a deck that was previously treated with a silicone or wax water repellent — Thompson's WaterSeal is the usual culprit — which leaves a residue that repels the new coat. If the previous finish is unknown and the water test beads, the deck needs stripping before anything new goes on; see <a href="/can-you-stain-over-old-deck-stain/">can you stain over old deck stain</a> for how to tell.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Old or Badly Stored Product</h3>
        <p>
          An oil stain that has been sitting half-full in a garage for three summers may have partly oxidised in the can, lost solvent, or separated so thoroughly that no amount of stirring recombines it. It goes on thick and gummy and cures unevenly. A water-based stain that has frozen and thawed has broken its emulsion and will never form a proper film. If the can was old, lumpy, stringy or smelled wrong, that may be the whole story.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A Second Coat on a One-Coat Product</h3>
        <p>
          Some products are formulated for one coat: apply until the wood stops absorbing, wipe the excess, done. Put a conventional second coat on one of these the next day and the wood is already full. The second coat has nowhere to go, and because these formulas are typically high in non-drying or slow-drying oils they stay soft indefinitely. Check the can; the product-by-product guidance is in <a href="/how-many-coats-of-deck-stain/">how many coats of deck stain</a>. The same applies to a second coat applied too soon on a two-coat product where the first coat is still wet.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dew or Rain Soon After Application</h3>
        <p>
          Rain within a few hours of staining beads the surface, dilutes water-based products and leaves oil stains spotted and cloudy. Less obvious but almost as common is dew. A deck finished at five in the evening on a clear night will be wet with condensation by midnight, and that moisture sits on the curing film until mid-morning. Oil stains under dew go milky and stay soft; water-based stains can blush white. Finish staining early enough that the surface is touch-dry before evening, and check the forecast for dew point as well as rain.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shade and North-Facing Sections</h3>
        <p>
          Very often the deck is fine except for one area — under the pergola, along the north wall, the section shaded by the maple. Those spots are cooler, damper and stiller, and they were probably wetter to begin with. They take longer to dry and they were more likely to be over-applied, because stain does not flash off in the shade and the applicator did not notice the surface staying wet. If only the shaded parts are tacky, you have your diagnosis.
        </p>

        <h2 id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Diagnosis: Is It Actually a Problem Yet?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before reaching for the rags, be sure the deck is genuinely late. Impatience causes almost as many problems as over-application, because people start wiping and re-coating a deck that just needed another day.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Oil-based penetrating stains</strong> are normally dry to the touch in 24 to 48 hours in warm, dry weather and ready for furniture and normal foot traffic at 72 hours. In cooler or humid conditions, 72 hours to touch-dry is not unusual, and full hardness can take a week. <strong>Water-based stains</strong> are typically touch-dry in 4 to 24 hours and take light traffic the next day, with full cure over a few days. <strong>Solid stains</strong> behave like paint: touch-dry in a few hours, recoatable in 24, fully hard in a few days. The specific numbers by product are in <a href="/deck-stain-drying-time/">deck stain drying time</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          So the rule of thumb: <strong>if an oil stain is still tacky after 72 hours with daytime temperatures above 60 degrees, low humidity and some sun, something is wrong and waiting is unlikely to fix it.</strong> For a water-based stain, the equivalent threshold is about 48 hours. Before that, particularly if the weather has been cool or damp, give it time and airflow.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then look at how it is failing, because that tells you which fix to start with. A faint tack over the whole deck with no shine, no pooling and no transfer to a white rag rubbed across it is a surface that is nearly there and needs a couple more warm days. A surface that is shiny, that shows a fingerprint, that transfers colour to a rag, or that has dark wet-looking pools along the board edges and around fastener heads is a surface with excess stain on it, and it will not cure until the excess is removed. A surface that is soft and rubbery rather than oily is over-applied water-based product. And a surface that is tacky only in the shade, or only where the sprayer started and stopped, is telling you exactly where to work.
        </p>

        <h2 id="fixes" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Fixes, In Order
        </h2>

        <p>
          Work down this list and stop when the deck dries. Most cases are resolved by the second step.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix One: Sun, Airflow and Time</h3>
        <p>
          If you are inside the normal window or only just past it, and the tack is faint, the cheapest fix is patience with help. Move anything that is blocking air or sun off the deck. Set up a box fan or two to move air across the surface; even a light breeze roughly halves the time a thin film needs. If it has been humid, wait for the dry day. Keep everyone off it, including the dog, because footprints in a nearly-cured oil finish are permanent. Give it two more warm, sunny days. A faint surface haze on an otherwise properly applied deck will very often be gone by then.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix Two: Wipe Off the Excess With Solvent-Dampened Rags</h3>
        <p>
          This is the classic fix and it works on the great majority of sticky decks. The excess stain is sitting on the surface; take it off. For an oil-based stain, dampen a lint-free cotton rag with mineral spirits — dampen, not soak — and rub the tacky area firmly along the grain. The solvent softens the semi-cured film, the rag lifts it, and you will see it come away brown. Turn the rag frequently and change it when it is loaded. Work board by board and blend into the surrounding area so you do not leave a visible edge. Follow with a dry rag. The wood keeps the stain that penetrated, which is what you wanted in the first place, and the surface is left with a very thin residual film that can actually cure. In our experience this alone rescues decks that people had assumed needed stripping. Give it 24 hours and check again.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix Three: Re-Wet With Fresh Stain and Wipe Off</h3>
        <p>
          When the excess has partly skinned over and gone gummy rather than liquid, a rag alone may skate over it. The trick here is to apply a very thin coat of the same fresh stain over the sticky area. The solvent in the fresh product re-dissolves the gummy layer and turns the whole thing back into a wet, mobile film. Let it sit for five to ten minutes, no longer, then wipe the entire area off with clean dry rags as if you were back-wiping on the day. You are not adding a coat; you are using the new stain as a solvent that carries pigment, so the colour stays even. This works especially well with paraffinic oils like Ready Seal and TWP, where the manufacturers themselves recommend it. Wipe thoroughly, because anything left behind is the same problem again.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix Four: Mineral Spirits Scrub for Stubborn Patches</h3>
        <p>
          For pooled stain that has been sitting for a week or more, or for board edges and fastener holes where the excess has gone thick, step up. Pour a little mineral spirits directly onto the patch, let it sit for a minute to soften, then scrub with a stiff nylon brush along the grain and immediately wipe up the slurry with rags. Repeat until the rag comes up mostly clean. This will lift some pigment as well as the excess, so the patch may end up slightly lighter than its surroundings; a thin coat of fresh stain wiped on and off after it has dried will even that out. Do this on a warm day with the deck in shade so the solvent does not flash off before it has worked.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fix Five: Strip and Start Again</h3>
        <p>
          If, after the wipe-down and the solvent scrub, the deck is still tacky a week later, the stain has failed for a reason that wiping cannot reach: it was applied over a film it could not bond to, the product was bad, or the wood was wet underneath and is still pushing moisture out. At that point the honest answer is to strip it. A sodium hydroxide deck stripper followed by a brightener takes off a failed penetrating stain in an afternoon, and once the deck has dried for 48 hours you can do the water test, confirm the wood is open, and re-stain properly. The process is in <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">how to strip deck stain</a>. It is a lost weekend, but it is a great deal better than living with a deck that collects every leaf and footprint for the next two years and then peels.
        </p>

        <h2 id="water-based" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Water-Based Stain That Will Not Dry
        </h2>

        <p>
          Water-based products fail differently and need a different approach. Because acrylics form a film as they dry, an over-applied water-based stain does not stay oily; it stays soft and rubbery, and it can trap water underneath a skin that looks dry. Mineral spirits will not touch it once it has begun to coalesce.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the stain is still wet — within a day or so of application — you can remove the excess with rags dampened in water, or with a little of the manufacturer's recommended cleaner, working quickly along the grain. Once it has skinned, the fix is to wash the deck with a mild detergent or a deck cleaner, scrub off anything loose, rinse, and let it dry completely for two or three warm days. A soft film will usually harden once the trapped water has finally left. If it is still soft after that, the film has failed and needs to come off with a stripper formulated for acrylics, which is a heavier product than the one used on oils. Once the deck is clean and dry, recoat thin — a water-based stain wants to be applied as a thin, even coat with immediate back-brushing, never flooded, and a second thin coat as the can directs.
        </p>

        <p>
          The one water-based failure that waiting will never cure is stain that froze in the can or was applied to a deck that then dropped below 40 degrees overnight. The emulsion breaks, the acrylic will not fuse, and the result is a chalky or gummy layer that must be stripped. If that is what happened, skip straight to the stripper. The chemistry differences between the two families, and why they fail differently, are in <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-deck-stain/">oil-based vs water-based deck stain</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Safety: Rags, Solvent and Fire
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every fix above involving an oil stain produces a pile of rags soaked in linseed, alkyd or similar drying oil, and this is the part of the job that can burn your house down. Drying oils cure by oxidation, and oxidation releases heat. A single rag laid flat sheds that heat harmlessly. A pile of rags, or a bundle stuffed into a bin or a plastic bag, insulates itself, the temperature climbs, and within a few hours the pile can reach its ignition point with no spark involved. Fire departments attribute a steady number of garage and deck fires every year to exactly this, usually the night after a staining job.
        </p>

        <p>
          The rule is simple. As you finish with each rag, either lay it flat and separate outdoors on concrete or gravel — not on the deck, not on the lawn — until it has dried stiff, or hang it over a fence or line, or drop it into a metal can filled with water and fit a tight lid. Never leave them in a heap, never put them in a plastic bag, and never bring them into the garage. Once they are dry and stiff they can go in the household trash. Treat the brushes and the mineral-spirits rags the same way; the solvent itself is flammable and the oil residue in a solvent-soaked rag is just as capable of self-heating.
        </p>

        <p>
          Mineral spirits also wants ventilation, gloves that it does not dissolve (nitrile is fine, latex is not), and eye protection when you are scrubbing. Keep it away from any pilot light or grill, and pour the used solvent into a labelled metal container for your local hazardous waste collection rather than down a drain or onto the lawn.
        </p>

        <h2 id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. Prevention Checklist
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every cause above has a matching habit that prevents it. Run through this before the next can is opened.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Do the water test.</strong> Sprinkle water on a clean, dry board. If it soaks in within a minute, the wood is open and ready. If it beads, the wood is closed — by mill glaze, an old finish or moisture — and stain will bead too. Clean and brighten, or strip, until the water goes in.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Confirm the wood is dry.</strong> At least 48 hours of dry weather after washing or rain, longer for shaded decks, and a moisture meter reading under 15 per cent if you own one.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Watch the weather for 24 hours either side.</strong> Air and surface temperature between 50 and 85 degrees, humidity below about 70 per cent, no rain and no heavy dew forecast overnight. Stain in the shade or on an overcast day, not on boards hot to the touch.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Apply what the wood will take and no more.</strong> On a penetrating stain, the target is a surface that looks evenly wet and then evenly absorbs within a few minutes. Shine that persists is excess. If in doubt, put on less; a thin coat that cured beats a thick one that did not.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Back-brush every board within minutes,</strong> whatever you applied with. On dense wood and one-coat products, back-wipe with a rag after fifteen to twenty minutes as well. If you sprayed, this is not optional; the trade-offs are in <a href="/brush-vs-roller-vs-sprayer-deck-stain/">brush vs roller vs sprayer</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Read the coat instructions and believe them.</strong> One-coat products get one coat. Two-coat products get the second coat inside the window the can gives, usually wet-on-wet within an hour or so, not the next day.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Use fresh product from one batch,</strong> stirred thoroughly, and never anything that has frozen or that has skinned over in the can.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Start early enough to finish early.</strong> The surface should be touch-dry before evening dew settles. On a big deck that means starting in the morning, not after lunch.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Adding another coat to fix a sticky one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The wood is already full. A second coat sits on top of the first and doubles the problem. Remove excess first; very often no more stain is needed at all.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Waiting weeks for a pooled oil stain to cure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A thick film of oil stain skins over and seals the wet layer beneath. It collects dirt, stays soft and eventually peels. Wipe it off in the first week while it is still mobile.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Spraying without back-brushing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A sprayer lays stain on unevenly and heavily. Without a brush behind it, every overlap and every low spot becomes a sticky patch.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Staining over a deck that is still damp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Stain cannot enter cells full of water, and the water leaving over the following days pushes the finish back up. Two dry days after washing, minimum.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Piling up the oily rags</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Drying oils self-heat as they cure and a bundled pile can ignite overnight. Lay rags flat outdoors to dry stiff, or drown them in a lidded metal can of water.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my deck stain still sticky after 3 days?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Almost always because more stain was applied than the wood could absorb. Penetrating stain cures by soaking into the fibre; whatever cannot get in sits on the surface as a film that oil-based products in particular cannot harden. Cold weather, high humidity, damp wood, mill glaze and a previous finish all reduce absorption and make the same over-application worse.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should deck stain take to dry?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Oil-based penetrating stains are normally dry to the touch in 24 to 48 hours and fully cured in 72 hours in warm, dry weather. Water-based stains are usually touch-dry in 4 to 24 hours. If a deck is still tacky after 72 hours with temperatures above 60 degrees Fahrenheit and low humidity, waiting longer will not fix it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you fix deck stain that won't dry?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Wipe off the excess. Dampen clean rags with mineral spirits for an oil stain, or water for a water-based one, and rub the sticky areas firmly until the surface stops transferring. If the film has partly skinned over, apply a very thin coat of fresh stain to re-wet it, wait a few minutes, and wipe everything off. Stubborn patches get a mineral-spirits scrub with a stiff brush. If it still will not cure, strip and redo.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sticky deck stain eventually dry on its own?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A thin surface haze often does, given a week of sun and airflow. A genuinely wet or gummy layer usually does not. It collects dirt and pollen, stays soft under foot, and eventually peels in sheets because it never bonded to the wood. Wiping it off is far quicker than hoping.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a second coat on sticky deck stain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No. If the first coat is tacky the wood is already saturated and the second coat has nowhere to go. It will sit on top of the first and make the problem worse. Remove the excess first, let the deck cure, then decide whether it needs any more product at all. Very often it does not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does humidity stop deck stain from drying?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It slows it considerably. Oil stains cure by oxidation and solvent evaporation, and both slow down in cool, humid, still air. Water-based stains cannot release their water into air that is already saturated. Staining late in the day, in shade, or when dew is due that night traps moisture in the film. Stain when there is a run of warm, dry days.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What do I do with rags soaked in oil stain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Never leave them piled up. Oil-based stains cure by oxidation, which generates heat, and a bundle of oily rags can heat itself to ignition in a few hours. Lay them flat outdoors on concrete or hang them on a line to dry stiff, or drop them into a metal can of water with a tight lid. Dispose of dried rags with household waste.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop deck stain from staying sticky next time?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Apply only what the wood absorbs and back-brush every board within a few minutes. Stain a dry deck in the 50 to 85 degree range with no rain or dew forecast for 24 hours, work in the shade, do a water test first so you know the wood is open, and never add a second coat to a product that specifies one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

